 then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $6\\times 10$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "900f650b-6d73-5995-901b-d7a17074a6aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522756 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=10,$ and since $B$ is formed by $4$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =10 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =10-4 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $4\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1b6e064-faca-5061-955a-61c19c8be97d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522771 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $A$ is not invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c579df31-38e7-5faa-a719-e9a3badf209e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522781 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4,$ then, by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $9\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=4$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3fc6c03-9e92-5752-b628-3af41a4a428b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522797 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
